# HERMES-AGENT — runtime variables
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS FIRST
#
# This file is a reference, not a configuration file to deploy. Nothing here
# is read from the working tree at runtime.
#
# The platform distinguishes two moments at which a value enters the system.
#
#   Compile time. Ports, addresses, model identifiers, caps, thresholds. They
#   are declared in parameters.nix, they end up in the Nix store, and they are
#   documented in the variable tables in README.md. None of them belong here.
#
#   Runtime. The values below. Every one of them is a secret. They are held by
#   the secret store, rendered by its agent into the runtime secrets directory
#   — a tmpfs, so they never touch the disk or the Nix store — and read by the
#   services from there through EnvironmentFile.
#
# The consequence is the rule this file exists to state: what you write next
# to a name below is the PATH in the secret store, never the value. A secret
# committed once is a secret to rotate, and the repository keeps history.
#
# The environment files are produced by modules/secrets-agent.nix. The listing
# below documents which service receives which variable, and from which path.
# Populate the paths with:
#
#     bao kv put <mount>/<path> <key>=@-        # value supplied on stdin
#
# Passing a secret as a command argument makes it visible in the process
# table. On a shared host, always use stdin.
#
# <mount> is hermes.secretStore.mount, and for this site it is `hermes`: every
# path written as <mount>/x below is hermes/x in the store. The complete list,
# with the command that creates each one, is at the end of this file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ===========================================================================
# Agent runtime — rendered to <runtime>/hermes-core.env
# Consumed by the interactive plane
# ===========================================================================

# Internal broker token for the interactive plane. Despite the name, this is
# NOT a gateway credential: it is a low-sensitivity token, valid on this host
# alone, that the broker exchanges for the real key. The name follows the
# OpenAI-compatible convention the runtime expects, and it is the variable
# somebody will eventually try to "repair" by putting a real key here. The
# policy attached to this identity is what stops that from working.
# Path: <mount>/broker/tokens, key "interactive"
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Application key of the memory backend. It authenticates and does not
# authorise: whoever holds it reaches every bank, which is why the separation
# between profiles rests on the bank identifier and on the network
# segmentation rather than on this credential.
# Path: <mount>/memory/tenant_key, key "key"
HINDSIGHT_API_KEY=

# Token used against the skill registries. It exists for the rate limits and
# carries no privilege of its own.
# Path: <mount>/skills/github_token, key "token"
GITHUB_TOKEN=


# ===========================================================================
# Agent runtime — rendered to <runtime>/hermes-svc.env
# Consumed by the programmatic plane
# ===========================================================================

# Internal broker token for the programmatic plane. Distinct from the
# interactive one: distinct token, distinct upstream credential, distinct
# spending counter. This separation is what makes the isolation between the
# two planes measurable rather than asserted.
# Path: <mount>/broker/tokens, key "programmatic"
# (same variable name as above, in a different environment file)
# OPENAI_API_KEY=


# ===========================================================================
# Egress broker — rendered to <runtime>/broker-tokens.json
#                 and <runtime>/broker-credentials.json
# ===========================================================================

# One internal token per profile, so that spend is attributable per profile
# without adding a header to the protocol. Generated by the profile
# provisioning unit at first run.
# Path: <mount>/broker/tokens/<profile>, key "value"

# Shared token for the memory extraction channel. This channel scales with
# total turns rather than with users, so it is a structural second cost
# channel and not an occasional one. It is labelled separately and is part of
# the cost perimeter.
# Path: <mount>/broker/tokens, key "extraction"

# Shared token for the evaluators. Labelled as evaluation spend, which is
# excluded from the cost-per-task figure: it is an instrument of measurement,
# spend ON the task rather than spend OF the task.
# Path: <mount>/broker/tokens, key "eval"

# The real gateway credentials. These exist only inside the broker process.
# No other identity has a policy that can read them, and the negative test
# that proves it runs before any application is deployed.
# Path: <mount>/inference/interactive, key "key"
# Path: <mount>/inference/programmatic, key "key"
# Path: <mount>/inference/extraction, key "key"


# ===========================================================================
# Memory platform — rendered to <runtime>/pg.env
# ===========================================================================

# Password of the database role backing the knowledge store.
# Path: <mount>/db/hindsight, key "password"
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=


# ===========================================================================
# Memory platform — rendered to <runtime>/hindsight.env
# ===========================================================================

# Tenant key the backend validates incoming requests against. Enabled
# explicitly: the backend ships with authentication available but switched
# off, and that must be proven on rather than assumed on.
# Path: <mount>/memory/tenant_key, key "key"
HINDSIGHT_API_TENANT_API_KEY=

# Access key of the memory inspection console, reachable only through the
# proxy and only by operators.
# Path: <mount>/memory/cp_key, key "key"
HINDSIGHT_CP_ACCESS_KEY=

# Internal broker token used by the extraction model. The extraction calls go
# through the broker like every other call: routing them directly would make
# the second cost channel invisible.
# Path: <mount>/broker/tokens, key "extraction"
HINDSIGHT_API_LLM_API_KEY=


# ===========================================================================
# Identity provider — rendered to <runtime>/authelia/{jwt,session,storage}
# ===========================================================================

# Signing key for the identity assertions.
# Path: <mount>/authelia/jwt_secret, key "value"
AUTHELIA_JWT_SECRET=

# Session signing key. Rotating it invalidates every active session, so plan
# it outside working hours.
# Path: <mount>/authelia/session_secret, key "value"
AUTHELIA_SESSION_SECRET=

# Encryption key of the local identity store, which holds the registered
# second factors.
# Path: <mount>/authelia/storage_key, key "value"
AUTHELIA_STORAGE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=


# ===========================================================================
# Chat interface — rendered to <runtime>/open-webui.env
# ===========================================================================

# Session secret of the chat interface. The interface has no login form of its
# own: the identity provider is the only way in, and this key only protects
# the session it establishes afterwards.
# Path: <mount>/ui/webui_secret, key "value"
WEBUI_SECRET_KEY=


# ===========================================================================
# Ingress — rendered to <runtime>/profile-bearers.conf
# ===========================================================================

# One bearer per profile, resolved by the proxy from the authenticated
# identity and injected on the way to the API server. The client never sees
# them and never supplies them. Generated by the profile provisioning unit.
# Path: <mount>/profiles/<profile>/bearer, key "value"


# ===========================================================================
# Evaluation platform — rendered to <runtime>/phoenix.env
# ===========================================================================

# Secret of the evaluation platform. It stays populated even though the
# platform's own authentication is disabled in favour of the single identity
# provider: an unset secret on a service that expects one is a default, not a
# decision.
# Path: <mount>/observability/phoenix_secret, key "value"
PHOENIX_SECRET=

# Internal broker token used by the evaluators. They call the broker, never
# the gateway: the containment of the inference credential admits no exception
# for evaluators either.
# Path: <mount>/eval/token, key "value"
EVAL_TOKEN=


# ===========================================================================
# Bootstrap credentials — NOT rendered by the agent
# ===========================================================================
#
# The credentials with which each guest proves its identity to the secret
# store are the one exception: they cannot come from the store, because they
# are what opens it. They are encrypted with sops, versioned under secrets/,
# and decrypted at system activation into the runtime secrets directory.
#
# Path in the repository: secrets/<host>.yaml, keys openbao/<identity>/role_id
#                         and openbao/<identity>/secret_id


# ===========================================================================
# The paths, in full, and the command that creates each one
#
# The registry the platform verifies itself against is policies/default.nix
# (expectedPaths). A path declared and never populated produces no
# configuration error: it produces the failure of whichever step reads it, and
# when that step is the negative test proving that the agentic guest cannot
# read an inference credential, the failure reads as evidence that the
# invariant holds. That is the reason this list exists in full.
#
# Run these on the secret store guest, after unsealing, before F-04.
# ===========================================================================
#
# ONE SECRET, FOUR KEYS — read this before the rest.
#
# hermes/broker/tokens holds the internal tokens of the two planes, of the
# extraction channel and of the evaluators, as four keys of a SINGLE secret. A
# `kv put` REPLACES the secret: writing the keys one at a time leaves only the
# last one, and the three services whose keys were erased fail on their next
# render cycle rather than at the moment of the mistake. Write all four in one
# operation, and use `kv patch` for a later rotation of one of them.
#
#   bao kv put hermes/broker/tokens - <<'JSON'
#   {"interactive":"…","programmatic":"…","extraction":"…","eval":"…"}
#   JSON
#
# Suitable values: head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64 -w0
#
# The three gateway credentials, readable by the broker identity alone:
#
#   bao kv put hermes/inference/interactive   key=@-
#   bao kv put hermes/inference/programmatic  key=@-
#   bao kv put hermes/inference/extraction    key=@-
#
# Three distinct keys on two distinct workspaces of the gateway: this is what
# makes the cost separation between the planes verifiable instead of asserted.
#
# Memory platform:
#
#   bao kv put hermes/db/hindsight            password=@-
#   bao kv put hermes/memory/tenant_key       key=@-
#   bao kv put hermes/memory/cp_key           key=@-
#
# Identity provider and chat interface:
#
#   bao kv put hermes/authelia/jwt_secret     value=@-
#   bao kv put hermes/authelia/session_secret value=@-
#   bao kv put hermes/authelia/storage_key    value=@-
#   bao kv put hermes/ui/webui_secret         value=@-
#
# Evaluation platform. The secret stays populated even though the platform's
# own authentication is disabled: an unset secret on a service that expects one
# is a default, not a decision.
#
#   bao kv put hermes/observability/phoenix_secret value=@-
#   bao kv put hermes/eval/token                   value=@-
#
# Skill registries — rate limits only, no privilege of its own:
#
#   bao kv put hermes/skills/github_token      token=@-
#
# NOT written by hand, and this matters:
#
#   hermes/profiles/<profile>/bearer          key "value"
#   hermes/broker/tokens/<profile>            key "value"
#
# One pair per profile — usr-a, usr-b, svc-daily-digest here. They are created
# by the provisioning unit of modules/hermes-profiles.nix, which creates the
# profile, its memory bank and both credentials as one idempotent operation. A
# profile created by hand works, and is still a defect: the bank template is
# the tenancy boundary, and a profile that skipped the unit can share a bank
# with another without producing any error.
#
# Verify completeness against the registry rather than by remembering the list.
# What is checked is presence, not content: reading the values back to compare
# them would put every secret of the platform in one shell history.
#
#   while read -r p; do
#     bao kv metadata get "hermes/$p" >/dev/null 2>&1 || echo "missing: $p"
#   done < "$(nix build .#baoPolicies --print-out-paths)/expected-paths.txt"
